        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Hi, first of all thanks for considering and all of mini.nvim.\n\nI use neovim at work and as work is I usually start on some changes in our codebase and then this gets interrupted by something with higher priority or somebody has a quick question and I want to look at other files. Or simply I need to wait on a response from someone before I can complete something.\n\nThe way I use tabs is then I have one tab for one of these \"contexts\" of work. For example:\n\n- Develop new Feature A -> Tab 1\n- Fix High Priority Bug B -> Tab 2 (interrupted Work in Tab 1)\n- Get a question in a meeting and open the corresponding files to show something -> Tab 3\n\nNow This works fine up to a point. But now I am constantly around 5-7 tabs and it  gets hard to keep in mind which tab is for what.\n\nFor this reason I implemented a way to name the tabs and persist this in the session (with mini.sessions). Basically I just set a tab local variable like `vim.t.tabname`. Now I would like to show this variable on the top instead of the `Tab 1/2`.\nThis way I could name my tabs like `Develop new Feature A` etc.\n\nThank you and no worries if you don't want to include it, then I will drop mini.tabline and roll my own. I just figured it would be a (technical) simple change though I understand it might not be a concept wise simple change. So no pressure!\n\nGreetings\nStefan",
